==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷelH-}}
The term was coined in 1848 by {{w|Rudolf Virchow}}. From {{der|en|fro|embolisme||intercalation of days in a calendar to correct errors}}, from {{der|en|LL.|embolismus}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἐμβολισμός||intercalary}}, from {{m|grc|ἐμβάλλω||to insert, throw in}}.<ref>[https://www.etymonline.com/search?q=embolism ''embolism (n.)'' at Etymonline.com]</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛmbəlɪzəm/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-embolism.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s}}

# {{lb|en|pathology}} An obstruction or occlusion of a [[blood vessel]] by an [[embolus]], that is by a [[blood clot]], air bubble or other matter that has been transported by the [[blood stream]].
# The insertion or [[intercalation]] of days into the [[calendar]] in order to correct the error arising from the difference between the [[civil year]] and the [[solar year]].
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Asiatic journal and monthly miscellany|year=1844|volume=2|passage=Authorities differ as to the manner in which the Arabs practised '''embolism''': some say, they added a month to every third year; others, that they intercalated seven months in a period of nineteen years ; and others, nine months in twenty-four years.|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=CihGAAAAcAAJ&dq=%22embolism%22%20intercalation&pg=PA167#v=onepage&q=%22embolism%22%20intercalation&f=false}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} An intercalated [[prayer]] for [[deliverance]] from [[evil]] coming after the [[Lord's Prayer]].
# {{lb|en|Roman Catholicism}} The variable [[body]] of a liturgical [[preface]], between the [[protocol]] and [[eschatocol]], typically stating the [[motive]] for worship on a given day.

